# Kairo keys and settings. Copy this file to .env.local beside it, then fill in what you have:
#
#     cp web/env.local.example web/.env.local
#
# One file serves both apps: the Next.js app in web/ loads .env.local natively, and the
# vanilla dashboard (node dashboard/server.js) loads the same file through dashboard/env.js.
# .env.local is gitignored (web/.gitignore: .env*.local), and so is every other dotenv
# file (root .gitignore: .env, .env.*). This template is deliberately named without the
# leading dot so that it falls outside those rules and can be committed — which also
# means it must never hold a real key.
#
# Everything here is optional. With no keys at all the dashboard still runs: job search,
# the rule-based CV tailoring, the Kanban tracker and the fit evaluator don't use a model.
# A key is what turns on the AI features — AI answers, AI CV edits, and the corrections
# loop that learns from your "Wrong answer? Fix it" corrections.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The AI model
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Gemini is tried first and is the one to set: the most generous free tier of the three,
# and the fastest to respond. A key is free and needs no card — https://aistudio.google.com/apikey
GEMINI_API_KEY=

# Optional. Which Gemini model to call. Leave unset for the default (gemini-3.5-flash-lite),
# which is verified working and answers in about a second; see the comment on GEMINI_MODEL
# in dashboard/cloud-llm.js for why some other Gemini model names hang or 404.
# GEMINI_MODEL=gemini-3.5-flash-lite

# Optional fallbacks, used only when Gemini is unset or its call fails (a free-tier rate
# limit, say). Groq wins if both are set. Either one alone also works on its own.
# Keys: https://console.groq.com/keys and https://openrouter.ai/keys
GROQ_API_KEY=
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=

# Optional. Override the model for whichever of those two you're using.
# GROQ_MODEL=openai/gpt-oss-120b
# OPENROUTER_MODEL=meta-llama/llama-3.3-70b-instruct:free

# Optional, and read only by the Next.js app in web/ (the vanilla dashboard doesn't use
# it): Claude for the "Tailor my CV" rewrite in web/lib/ai-tailor.ts. Either an API key
# or an OAuth token, not both.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local model (optional, no key needed)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Ollama running on your own machine, used as the last fallback when no cloud call
# succeeds. Install from https://ollama.com, then `ollama pull llama3.1:8b`.
# OLLAMA_URL=http://127.0.0.1:11434
# LOCAL_LLM_MODEL=llama3.1:8b

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deployment only — not needed to run locally
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Postgres for the Next.js app on Vercel, where the filesystem is read-only (see
# web/README.md). Locally the app reads and writes the repo's own files instead, so
# leave these unset. Vercel's Storage tab sets them for you.
# DATABASE_URL=
# POSTGRES_URL=
# Upstash Redis works as an alternative to Postgres; Postgres wins if both are set.
# KV_REST_API_URL=
# KV_REST_API_TOKEN=

# Optional self-hosted service URLs, both with working defaults.
# LATEX_API_URL=
# FREEHIRE_API_URL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Apify - LinkedIn, Glassdoor and Indeed (optional, billed per run)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Why: the built-in LinkedIn scraper works from a home IP but is throttled to almost
# nothing from a datacenter one. The same query measured 35 LinkedIn results locally and
# 1 on Vercel. Apify runs its actors through residential proxies, which is the part we
# can't reproduce ourselves, and adds Glassdoor and Indeed, which have no free API.
#
# Leave APIFY_TOKEN unset and nothing changes: the seven free boards behave exactly as
# before and no Apify board is registered.
# Get a token at https://console.apify.com/account/integrations
# APIFY_TOKEN=

# The actors. These defaults are AgentX's, chosen because all three take the same input
# (keyword, country, location, max_results, posted_since, remote_only), so one builder
# drives them. all-jobs-scraper covers Indeed plus the ATS boards - Greenhouse, Lever,
# Ashby and the rest of its 42 platforms - which is why there is no separate Indeed
# actor. Set one to an empty string to disable just that source.
# APIFY_LINKEDIN_ACTOR=agentx~linkedin-jobs-scraper
# APIFY_GLASSDOOR_ACTOR=agentx~glassdoor-jobs-scraper
# APIFY_ALLJOBS_ACTOR=agentx~all-jobs-scraper

# Swapping in a different actor usually means a different input shape. "bebity" is
# recognised by name (it takes titles/locations arrays instead of keyword/location
# strings), so this is only needed for an actor whose dialect can't be inferred.
# Values: agentx (default) or bebity
# APIFY_LINKEDIN_DIALECT=

# For an actor that wants something else entirely, give its input as raw JSON. The
# query, row count and date filter are merged in on top, so one stored input serves
# every search. Example: APIFY_GLASSDOOR_INPUT={"includeEnrichedData":true}
# APIFY_LINKEDIN_INPUT=
# APIFY_GLASSDOOR_INPUT=
# APIFY_ALLJOBS_INPUT=

# The AgentX actors require a country code and take a code, not a region. The app's own
# locations are regions ("Worldwide", "EMEA"), so there is nothing to derive it from -
# set the country you actually want to search. 98 values are accepted.
# APIFY_COUNTRY=us

# How long one run may take. The search route's own ceiling is 60s (maxDuration), so
# going much above 40 risks the whole search timing out rather than just this board.
# APIFY_TIMEOUT_S=40
# Rows per run. Each one is billed, and only the best ~20 per board are ever shown.
# APIFY_MAX_ITEMS=50
# Runs cost money, so repeated searches reuse the last one for this long.
# APIFY_CACHE_MINUTES=60
